n.\n51: \n52: ## Human identity and desktop RDP configuration (2026-10-22)\n53: \n54: - Owner explicitly chose restoring the existing human identity, not adopting the chatbot identity. OpenWrt `wgdecoy` now uses the saved human key, public key `0I5FNCuHAu62VctaNgOlJ7RgfejJpYe9Qd0VdPGesX8=`, alias `jeremy-wg-f036`, session `414fbc56-03fb-20ab-758a-bcc7a7cbdc0e`, principal `c9bdf32e-811d-2495-b688-277c9e5c4d36`. UCI and the live interface were updated. Decoy's old router peer was replaced with this key for `10.0.0.9/32` and persisted with `wg-quick save`.\n55: - Address `10.0.0.9/32`, endpoint `129.80.64.127:51821`, AllowedIPs `10.0.0.0/24`, and keepalive 25 remain unchanged. WireGuard still terminates on Decoy; none was installed on the VPS. The bridge's separate browser control-plane/chatbot binding was deliberately not changed to the human key.\n56: - Private rollback copies: router `/etc/config/network.before-human-identity-20261022T152114Z`; Decoy `/etc/wireguard/wg-opdbus.conf.before-human-identity-20261022T152114Z`. Keep these secret-bearing files private. No post-change handshake or authenticated browser reconnect was exercised.\n57: - Desktop Hypr-RDP 0.1.7 is configured in `/home/jeremy/.config/hypr-rdp/config.toml`, with PAM service `hypr-rdp` and listener `0.0.0.0:3389`. User unit `/home/jeremy/.config/systemd/user/hypr-rdp.service` is enabled for the graphical session and was started; service/listener and capture/input initialization were observed. This is a graphical-session service, not a claim that a usable desktop exists before login.\n58: - UFW admits TCP3389 from `10.0.0.0/24` only. OpenWrt's persistent `firewall.hypr_rdp_vpn` redirects WireGuard-zone traffic to `10.0.0.9:3389` to the desktop's wired `192.168.1.118:3389`, restricted to that source subnet, with no SNAT/reflection. Router firewall backup: `/etc/config/firewall.before-hypr-rdp-20261022T152351Z`.\n59: - Manual RDP check: from another WireGuard client use `10.0.0.9:3389`, username `jeremy`, the desktop Linux password, and TLS without NLA. Actual remote-desktop authentication, post-change VPN traversal, and reboot persistence have not been exercised.\n60: \n61: ## Live release-reference recovery (2026-10-22)\n62: \n63: - Native deployment refused to retire previous release root315 with `EBUSY`, before unlink or cutover. The first incoming transfer was cleanly aborted through `op-release-launch abort`; active B/root454, previous A/root315, complete readiness and the original bridge supervisor selection were restored. The repaired bridge artifact was retained for a fresh canonical deployment.\n64: - The WARP daemon launched by `cf-mesh` uses a private mount namespace. It retained old release overlays and copies of the host FUSE mounts. Restarting it alone released the old overlays but copied those same old FUSE views again, so it did not resolve the remaining references.\n65: - Host FUSE daemons4521/4614 still executed from the old release overlay. Their executables were byte-identical to the committed active binary. Both host views were normally unmounted and remounted using their existing fstab adapter, preserving stores, human session and call-owner UID. No force/lazy unmount or daemon kill was used.\n66: - **Ordering matters:** refresh both host FUSE views first, then restart `cf-mesh` to release its copies of the old views. An old FUSE daemon can legitimately remain alive after the host unmount while WARP's private namespace retains another mount. After the ordered refresh, both old daemons exited naturally (`refresh-fuse-aliases.receipt`, exit0, 23:07:15Z).\n67: - Container FUSE devices194/195 were distinct from the old host devices52/53 and were not touched. No Incus/container restart, reboot, kernel/tool installation, filesystem-guard weakening or storage-service activation occurred. Operational receipts are under `/var/tmp/audit-durability-20261022.vPlgbW/`; browser/VPN reconnection remains a manual check.\n68: \n69: ## Registered human session repair (2026-10-23, `door-session-repair-20261022`)\n70: \n71: - **Reproduced cause:** the real deployed UI obtained fresh OIA1 through the existing router WireGuard path and completed both bootstrap calls, then SID1 failed with `sealed identity has no matching provisioned container binding`. Mounted `identity_sled.get_own_identity` confirmed the existing human record intentionally has no native instance or Btrfs device. This is distinct from the old chatbot-assignment failure above.\n72: - **Repair:** `identity_session_binding_matches` in `crates/op-grpc-bridge/src/identity_sled_dispatch.rs` now applies the existing containerless-human rule to SID1 authentication, credential refresh and genesis-stamp validation. Any attached instance must still match exactly; service and unclassified identities cannot omit their instance. Registry/revocation, credential commitment, identity, transport, expiry and grants remain enforced. No identity, key, grant, container or storage membership was created or changed.\n73: - **Deployment:** one locked native GNU release build completed at 00:09:16Z; canonical `activate-golden.sh` completed at 00:15:07Z. The reviewed regression-only fixture correction followed the build and does not change production code. Durable build, activation and admission receipts: `/var/tmp/door-session-repair-20261022.q6JCuh/`. Before activation, both host FUSE views were refreshed normally, then `cf-mesh` restarted to release its retained old namespace; the old daemons exited naturally. No force/lazy unmount, daemon kill or container restart.\n74: - **Post-deploy UI evidence:** main-host Chromium running the deployed UI, with unchanged fresh issuer documents relayed in memory through the existing router WG interface, received HTTP200/CORS for the correct origin and three canonical CallMethod responses with gRPC status0. The credential rejection is gone. This is not a capture of the user's browser.\n75: - **Provider configuration restored:** the running bridge selected missing `/etc/op-dbus/model-providers.toml`; admitted read operation `b2a4f48b-95c4-4a17-87a9-ef7044062671` had unknown outcome. Mounted `tched_fs.get_receipt` was denied missing `cap.storage.3tchedfs.receipt.read@v1`; no alternate identity, database read or grant change bypassed that denial. Archive inspection found only a symlink to retired `/opt/op-dbus/current/etc/op-dbus/model-providers.toml`, not independent mutable settings. At 00:21:35Z the documented tracked `deploy/config/model-providers.toml` was installed only into the absent runtime path, root:root/0644, with identical SHA256 `a3d5a93ea3f5e092222900c00e2808967a1d31714826c137a16925164353f9e6`. Existing key references and disabled-provider settings were retained; no key contents or services changed. Rollback is removal of this newly created file only while it still matches that hash.\n76: - **Connected surface verified:** a fresh deployed-UI connection after restoring the prerequisite reaches `Connected`, shows the original human session's Personal namespace, loads the provider picker and reports `Ready`. This was a new bounded verification of the read path after repair, not an automatic replay of the unresolved operation. `connected-ui.json` and `connected-settings.webp` retain the evidence. No model was applied or inference submitted. Separate visible limits: Knowledge feed reconnects with socket code1006; `opencode_go` and `voyageai` refresh failures are reported with stored models retained. The browser navigation's network-idle wait timed out while the connected UI remained live; direct surface inspection and screenshot establish the connection result, not that timed-out wait. The owner-authorized targeted credential regression started only after deployment and remains pending.\n77: \n78: ## Current network — owner correction (2026-10-20)\n79: \n80: ONLY SERVICE INGRESS ENDPOINT: 10.0.0.2:8090. EMQX IS NOT ON 10.0.0.3.  \n81: ONLY SERVICE INGRESS ENDPOINT: 10.0.0.2:8090. EMQX IS NOT ON 1